  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nA mid-level ridge centered over the northern Gulf Coast will \ncontinue to enforce hot and generally rain-free conditions through \nThursday. We'll see those streamer clouds moving northward daily, \nwith an occasional shower as the seabreeze pushes in, but moisture \nwill remain near or below normal for August. An inverted shortwave \ntrough will ride the southwestern edge of the ridge Friday, \nextending into South Texas. This will increase moisture to around \nthe 75th percentile (2.0\" PWAT) and with mid-level vorticity \nsupport, warrant mentionable shower and thunderstorm chances across \nthe Coastal Plains (20%). Enhanced moisture can linger into \nSaturday, and allow a few more showers and thunderstorms to develop. \nMid-level high pressure will move westward into the ARKLATEX region \nand promote another stretch of hot and mainly rain-free conditions.\n\nHeat will remain our primary concern across South Texas. Each \nafternoon will be flirting with Heat Advisory criteria, with peak \nheat indices of 110-115 but uncertainties on the duration. No matter \nwhat, it will be hot and precautions are necessary for the moderate \nto major risk of heat-related impacts intact. Heat safety measures \nare encouraged, please remember to look before you lock and limit \noutdoor exposure.",
